::Q1::A function is deployed to Lambda but nothing triggers it for a week. What happens? 
{
=Nothing runs and nothing is billed, since Lambda charges per invocation and execution time
~The function is billed at a reduced idle rate until it is invoked
~The function is automatically deleted after a period of inactivity
~The function runs on a default schedule to stay warm
}

::Q2::A Lambda handler receives two arguments. What does each one carry? 
{
=event describes what triggered the function and its details; context describes the invocation itself
~event carries the function's return value; context carries its configuration
~event holds the execution role; context holds the environment variables
~event is the raw HTTP request; context is the raw HTTP response
}

::Q3::Why does a function handling S3 events loop over event "Records" rather than reading a single object from the event? {
=A single invocation may cover more than one object, so assuming one file is a common bug
~The Records list always contains exactly one item, but the loop is a required convention
~Records holds the function's previous invocations for auditing
~Looping is required to keep the function within its execution time limit
}

::Q4::A Lambda function reads from one S3 bucket and writes to a database. Which set of permissions should its execution role include? {
=Read access to that one bucket, permission to connect to that one database, and permission to write logs
~Read access to that one bucket and permission to connect to that one database, with no other permissions
~Full administrative access, so the function is never blocked by a permissions error
~No role at all, with an access key stored in the function code instead
}

::Q5::Partners upload files at unpredictable times, and the data is needed within seconds of arrival. Which pipeline approach fits, and why? {
=Event driven, because the work is triggered by the upload itself rather than waiting for the next scheduled run
~Scheduled, because running at fixed times is more efficient for any volume of data
~Scheduled, because event driven pipelines cannot write to a database
~Either one, since latency is not affected by the choice
}

::Q6::A developer sets a counter variable during one Lambda invocation and expects to read it during the next. Why does this not work? {
=Invocations are stateless and share nothing, so anything that must survive has to be written to an external store
~The counter is reset only when the function code is redeployed
~Variables persist but are cleared whenever a cold start occurs
~The execution role denies write access to memory
}